    "['mpls te bandwidth [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for `mpls te bandwidth ct0 10000`:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Cisco, bandwidth is specified in Mbps (10 = 10000 Kbps)\\n2. Cisco doesn\\'t have explicit class-type (ct0) configuration in the basic bandwidth command - class-based bandwidth reservations are handled differently via DS-TE or other mechanisms\\n3. The value \"10000\" in Huawei (in Kbps) becomes \"10\" in Cisco (in Mbps)', 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te igp shortcut', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng autoroute announce', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for `mpls te igp shortcut`:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng autoroute announce\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command performs the same function as Huawei\\'s `mpls te igp shortcut`, which enables the MPLS TE tunnel to be used as a next hop in IGP routing calculations. The \"autoroute announce\" feature in Cisco allows the tunnel to be considered as a valid path in the routing table.', 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng autoroute announce'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te igp metric absolute [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng administrative-weight 1', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for `mpls te igp metric absolute 1`:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng administrative-weight 1\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command sets the IGP metric (administrative weight) for the MPLS TE tunnel to an absolute value of 1, which is the equivalent functionality to Huawei's `mpls te igp metric absolute 1`.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng administrative-weight 1'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te service-class [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth class-type 1 class-type 2', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for `mpls te service-class af1 af2`:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth class-type 1 class-type 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, AF1 corresponds to class-type 1 and AF2 corresponds to class-type 2 in the DiffServ-aware TE (DS-TE) model.\\n2. Cisco uses \"class-type\" terminology where Huawei uses \"service-class\" with AF values.\\n3. The command structure is different as Cisco places this under the tunnel interface configuration.', 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth class-type 1 class-type 2'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mode mpls traffic-eng', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ntunnel mode mpls traffic-eng\\n##\\n\\nFor Cisco devices, the equivalent command to \"tunnel-protocol mpls te\" in Huawei is \"tunnel mode mpls traffic-eng\". This sets the tunnel encapsulation to MPLS Traffic Engineering (TE). \\n\\nThe command would be used in interface configuration mode when setting up an MPLS TE tunnel interface, similar to how it\\'s used in the Huawei configuration.', 'trans_template': 'tunnel mode mpls traffic-eng'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te tunnel-id [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng tunnel-id 200', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for `mpls te tunnel-id 200`:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng tunnel-id 200\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command sets the MPLS TE tunnel identifier to 200, which matches the functionality of the Huawei command. The tunnel-id is used to uniquely identify the MPLS TE tunnel within the router's configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng tunnel-id 200'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te bandwidth [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10000', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"The equivalent Cisco command for HUAWEI's `mpls te bandwidth ct0 10000` would be:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10000\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, `tunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth` sets the bandwidth for the MPLS TE tunnel (similar to Huawei's CT0 class)\\n- The value `10000` is preserved (units are kbps in both cases)\\n- Note that Cisco doesn't use explicit class-types (CT0, CT1, etc.) in the basic bandwidth configuration - class-based reservations would require additional QoS configuration in Cisco.\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng bandwidth 10000'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te igp shortcut', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng autoroute announce', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for `mpls te igp shortcut`:\\n\\n##\\nmpls traffic-eng autoroute announce\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco command `mpls traffic-eng autoroute announce` enables IGP shortcut functionality, which is the equivalent of Huawei's `mpls te igp shortcut`. Both commands allow the IGP to use the MPLS TE tunnel as a next hop in the routing table.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng autoroute announce'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te igp metric absolute [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng igp metric absolute 1', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for `mpls te igp metric absolute 1`:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng igp metric absolute 1\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command sets the IGP metric for the MPLS TE tunnel to an absolute value of 1, which is the same behavior as the Huawei command. The Cisco syntax places the MPLS TE configuration under the tunnel interface context rather than as a separate command.\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng igp metric absolute 1'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te service-class [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng signalling class-type 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nmpls traffic-eng signalling class-type 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Huawei's configuration, `mpls te service-class default` sets the service class to default (which typically corresponds to Class Type 0 in Cisco's terminology).\\n- The Cisco equivalent is `mpls traffic-eng signalling class-type 0`, where:\\n  - `class-type 0` represents the default service class\\n  - This command is used in the MPLS TE tunnel interface configuration context, just like in the Huawei configuration\", 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng signalling class-type 0'}]]": 1,
    "['ospf [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['opaque-capability [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng extensions igp', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nmpls traffic-eng extensions igp\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `opaque-capability enable` command enables OSPF to carry MPLS TE information in opaque LSAs. The equivalent Cisco IOS command is `mpls traffic-eng extensions igp`, which enables MPLS traffic engineering extensions for the IGP (OSPF in this case), allowing it to flood TE information.', 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng extensions igp'}]]": 1,
    "['enable traffic-adjustment [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng area [parameter1]', 'parent_command': ['router ospf [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router ospf [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['area [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n area 0\\n##\\n``` \\n\\nThe Huawei `area 0.0.0.0` command is equivalent to Cisco's `area 0` under OSPF configuration mode. Note that in Cisco:\\n1. The area ID can be expressed in decimal format (0) instead of dotted-decimal (0.0.0.0)\\n2. The command must be entered under `router ospf <process-id>` context first\\n3. I included the `router ospf 1` context line as Cisco requires entering OSPF configuration mode first\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n area 0\\n##\\n``` \\n\\nThe Huawei `area 0.0.0.0` command is equivalent to Cisco's `area 0` under OSPF configuration mode. Note that in Cisco:\\n1. The area ID can be expressed in decimal format (0) instead of dotted-decimal (0.0.0.0)\\n2. The command must be entered under `router ospf <process-id>` context first\\n3. I included the `router ospf 1` context line as Cisco requires entering OSPF configuration mode first\", 'trans_template': 'area 0'}]]": 1,
